Current original Zero workflow (0.12)

Use the native tools on Linux or their .exe versions on Windows:
  zero-integrate PROJECT_DIRECTORY NEW_KERNEL.zero
  zero-build PROJECT_DIRECTORY NEW_OUTPUT_DIRECTORY NEW_KERNEL.zero
  zero-check PROJECT_DIRECTORY NEW_TEST_DIRECTORY
All destination paths must be new. The integrator preserves authored sources;
the builder checks its compiler fixed point and builds the original targets;
the verifier checks language behavior and host file operations in process.
See tools/integrate.ztext, tools/build.ztext and checks/README.ztext.
Read these documents with zero-text read FILE.

The notes below preserve earlier implementation and test history. Any old
shell/Python runner names, generated artifacts, JSON reports or commands are
historical references: they were removed from the active project and are not
current runnable instructions or current-release validation. Native host
checks do not replace BIOS/UEFI, persistence or physical-hardware testing.

Historical implementation notes

Original Zero BIOS block move primitive

Candidate integration (host text editing only):
python3 portability/guest/biosmove/apply.py INPUT_COMPILER.zero OUTPUT_COMPILER.zero
Compile the output with the current own Zero compiler, then self-compile once
more and compare bytes. This task does not publish a live compiler itself.
Builtin index56/token556, template85 are reserved for biosmove; guest headers
use template84, and Windows uses existing host template overrides.

REAL-only value operation:
    :biosmove[0x6800 | 16384] -> @status.

The primitive invokes BIOS INT15/AH87 with ES:SI pointing to the caller's
48-byte descriptor table and CX containing the number of16-bit words. It
copies no policy or device logic into the compiler. Table must be a physical
low-memory address<=0xffd0; words must be1..32768. Invalid values return
0xffffffff without accessing the table or entering BIOS. A successful BIOS
call returns0. On CF error, nonzero AH is returned, or256 if BIOS setCF but
leftAH zero. Both expression arguments follow the existing left-to-right
Zero evaluation and binary operand convention.

Original93-byte register shim is stored as Zero template data, not assembly
input. It preserves general registers other than the EAX result, DS/ES and
EFLAGS, then clearsDF like the existing BIOS primitives. Temporary result
storage uses the existing CS:0x6e08 BIOS scratch. It enables interrupts for
the firmware call and restores the caller's interrupt state on completion.
The BIOS may alter A20 state; callers must reenable/verify A20 before directly
accessing high memory or entering protected/long mode.

Descriptor table owned by caller
Initialize all48 bytes before each call; firmware may modify its work entries.
Entries0/1 (bytes0..15): clear/reserved for BIOS.
Entry2 at+16: source data segment.
Entry3 at+24: destination data segment.
Entries4/5 (bytes32..47): BIOS work space.
For each source/destination descriptor:
  +0 u16 limit=byte_count-1 (up to65535)
  +2 u16 base bits0..15
  +4 u8  base bits16..23
  +5 u8  access0x93 (present writable data)
  +6 u8  flags/upper limit0
  +7 u8  base bits24..31
The shim does not validate descriptor contents, address ownership, usable RAM,
source/destination overlap or firmware support. The Zero boot loader supplies
those policies, including BIOS E820 checks for its target range.

Native verification
python3 portability/guest/biosmove/run.py [CANDIDATE_COMPILER]

probe.zero executes an actual BIOS optical read of32KiB into physical0x20000,
then performs two32-byte guard initializations and a32KiB payload copy at each
of4MiB,8MiB and32MiB. Host QMP memory reads independently compare every byte,
including the first/last payload bytes and both guards. Invalid argument cases
are checked inside the guest before real copies. All nine firmware calls
return0. The VM is stopped and no writable user disk is attached.

The32MiB test demonstrates that this tested SeaBIOS honors descriptor base
bits above24. It does not establish arbitrary4GiB support, an ISA guarantee
for every BIOS, native VirtualBox success, or physical-hardware compatibility.
The original AH87 interface historically includes24-bit-address BIOSes; the
planned4..5MiB bundle destination stays below that16MiB limit. Firmware failure
must be handled by the loader. Evidence: verification/biosmove/.
